  "query_preview": "..\n\nMain weather story of today is the landfall of Isaias last evening \nand its ongoing extratropical transition. While SE Michigan is \nspared of the bulk of this system, the shield of high cloud outside \nrepresents its northern edge. While moisture quality is expected to \ndegrade as the gulf moisture stream cuts off, models are still \ngenerating light rain potential Sunday with a thickening cloud deck \naloft. Diabatic cooling leads to a cooler day Sunday with \ntemperatures in the cloud shield holding below 70 degrees. Slightly \nwarmer temperatures expected further inland as the remnants of Isais \npivot east into the mid-Atlantic by Sunday afternoon. Much drier air \nthen fills into the Great Lakes Sunday night-Monday, which currently \nstretches from the southern Plains to the Upper Midwest. Strong \nsubsidence signature observed in 12z soundings (e.g. TOP/LMN/OUN) \nshould be the airmass that advects into SE Michigan Sunday night, \nwhich combined with a moistened boundary layer/longer mid-October \nnights could result in fog development Monday morning. \n\nDrier day than expected Monday as brief ridging slides overhead. \nWinds shift to the southwest with the passage of the ridge axis, \nquickly leading to warm advection into SE Michigan Monday. Highs \nthus warm into the upper 70s with highs possibly breaking 80 degrees \nTuesday. The catch to this is another opportunity for rain beginning \nlate Monday night and lingering through mid-week. This comes as \nmoisture pools along the deformation axis to our north, while return \nflow sets up to the south. Increasing LLJ winds Monday night bring \nin the initial wave of mid-level moisture, followed by increasing \nsynoptic support through the day Tuesday as the LLJ, surface \nlow/cold front, and finally a strong ~110 knot upper level jet work \ninto SE Michigan. While moisture depth is not overly impressive on \nits own, multiple lift mechanisms through the day will compensate \nfor this at least partially to keep rain chances going. \nThermodynamic profiles show a strong elevated mixed layer (mid level \nlapse rates ~7.0 C/km), so cannot rule out a few rumbles of thunder \nparticularly as the front comes through during peak heating. SWODY3 \nhas designated General Thunder for most of the state. \n\nPassage of the cold front establishes strong cold advection across \nthe region between 00z Wed thru 00z Thu. For reference the 10.06z \nECMWF has h8 temps dropping from 15 C to 7 C. This brings surface \ntemps back toward normal values for mid-October by mid-week (mid-60s \nhighs and mid 40s temps). This cooler airmass is likely to keeping \ncloud cover and possibly lake induced showers around through \nWednesday given still warm lake temperatures in the teens and N/NW \nflow. High pressure will eventually erode moisture ahead of a strong \nlow that draws even cooler temperatures into SE Michigan by next \nweekend.",
